.TH LINUXCNCTOP\-DUMP "1"  "2026-07-31" "LinuxCNC Documentation" "The Enhanced Machine Controller"
.SH NAME
linuxcnctop\-dump \- print the LinuxCNC machine status as text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linuxcnctop\-dump
[\fI\-\-json\fR] [\fI\-n SEC\fR] [\fI\-\-full\fR] [\fI\-\-instance NAME\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBlinuxcnctop\-dump\fR prints the current machine status to standard output and
exits. It is the headless counterpart of \fBlinuxcnctop\fR(1), for machines
without a display, for scripts, and for pasting state into a bug report.

The default output is one \fIname value\fR line per status field, using the
classic field names \fBlinuxcnc.stat()\fR always exposed. \fB\-\-json\fR prints
the raw status snapshot instead, whose field paths are the ones the
\fBlinuxcnctop\fR web application displays.

If the controller cannot be reached, the command reports the failure on standard
error and exits non\-zero rather than printing a stale or empty machine.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print the raw \fIStatFull\fR snapshot from the REST API as indented JSON.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-interval\fR \fISEC\fR
Repeat every \fISEC\fR seconds until interrupted. In this mode an unreachable
controller is reported and waited out rather than being fatal, so the command
can be started before the machine is up.
.TP
\fB\-\-full\fR
Do not truncate values to 58 columns (text mode only).
.TP
\fB\-\-instance\fR \fINAME\fR
Query the named task instance. Defaults to \fB$GMC_INSTANCE\fR, or
\fImilltask\fR if that is unset.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GMC_REST_URL
Base URL of the controller's REST API (default http://127.0.0.1:5080).
.TP
.B GMC_INSTANCE
Default task instance name.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the status once:
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop\-dump
.RE
.PP
Watch the status once a second, untruncated:
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop\-dump \-n 1 \-\-full
.RE
.PP
Capture the raw snapshot for a bug report:
.PP
.RS
linuxcnctop\-dump \-\-json > status.json
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBlinuxcnctop(1)\fR, \fBLinuxCNC(1)\fR

Much more information about LinuxCNC and HAL is available in the LinuxCNC
and HAL User Manuals, found at /usr/share/doc/LinuxCNC/.

